SUMMARY:The Space for Music - Jose Bevia Trio with special guest Sergio Albacete
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, October 14 | doors open at 7:30\, show starts at 8 pm | sliding scale tickets $25 – $30 \nCo-hosted by Seed Artists \nThe Space for Music with the Jose Bevia Trio and special guest Sergio Albacete\, visiting from Spain. \nJose Bevia on piano and keyboard\, Ronen Itzik on drums\, Peter Slavov on bass\, and Sergio Albacete on saxophone. \nThis program explores the intersections of Andalusian musical tradition\, contemporary jazz and improvisation\, tracing connections between Spain\, the United States\, and Cuba. Across the eight works\, traditional melodies and rhythmic practices are reframed through contemporary harmony\, metric exploration\, and spontaneous improvisation. \nThe concert opens with Mononas\, Sergio Albacete’s reharmonization of a traditional melody from Villanueva de la Reina in Jaén\, Andalusia. Characterized by its distinctive major-key sonority and an unchanging melodic structure\, the piece demonstrates how a traditional folk idiom can acquire new harmonic dimensions while retaining its essential identity. \nThe Smell of the Sunshine\, by José Beviá\, shifts the focus to the Finger Lakes region of New York. Inspired by the light\, landscape\, and tranquility of Cayuga Lake\, the composition combines influences from Ahmad Jamal\, Bill Evans\, and Bob Mintzer with unexpected metric shifts and evolving thematic material. \nIn Tangos de Cazorla\, Albacete draws on Andalusian folk traditions preserved in the mountainous regions of Cazorla and Segura. Incorporating the rhythms of tientos and tangos with the Andalusian cadence\, the arrangement later engages with the harmonic language of John Coltrane\, creating a dialogue between Andalusian tradition and American jazz. \nNico-Lico\, by José Beviá\, explores the relationship between composition and improvisation. Influenced by post-bop\, Latin music\, and contemporary compositional practices\, the work allows the soloist to shape the tempo and rhythmic flow\, making time itself an element of musical interaction. Written and improvised passages gradually merge into a unified formal process. \nEunidenserías\, by Sergio Albacete\, offers a contemporary perspective on the bulerías. Traditional flamenco rhythmic and melodic gestures encounter the metric and rhythmic concepts of modern jazz\, particularly those associated with musicians such as Ari Hoenig. \nSedona Valley\, by José Beviá\, is inspired by the dramatic landscapes of Sedona and the Grand Canyon. The composition translates the immense scale\, vivid colors\, and geological beauty of the American Southwest into a musical reflection on place and memory. \nNana del Caballo Grande\, based on the music of Camarón de la Isla and arranged by Sergio Albacete\, revisits one of the defining works of La Leyenda del Tiempo. The arrangement connects Camarón’s modern flamenco language with the historic form of the caña\, linking ancient Andalusian song traditions with the transformative aesthetics of 20th-century flamenco. \nThe program concludes with La Guarida\, José Beviá’s tribute to Cuban music and to a deeply personal moment in Havana. Inspired by a piano lesson with a Cuban musician\, the composition develops a montuno as a recurring subject\, transforming it through successive variations\, improvisation\, and bitonal passages. The piece ultimately becomes both a musical exploration and a personal narrative\, named for the Havana restaurant where Beviá proposed to his wife\, Maggie. \nBYOB is welcome\, some light refreshments will be offered. \n  \nSergio Albacete is a Spanish multi-instrumentalist\, composer\, arranger\, and researcher with a\ndistinguished 30-year career. He holds advanced studies in classical saxophone and a Master’s\nDegree in Jazz Performance from Musikene. He has performed and conducted Big Bands for 25\nyears and has been a tenured faculty member at the “Ramón Garay” Professional Conservatory\nsince 2004.\nAlbacete has collaborated and performed with internationally renowned musicians including\nBarry Harris\, Eric Alexander\, Kurt Elling\, Lee Konitz\, Jerry Bergonzi\, Perico Sambeat\, Marco\nMezquida\, Enrique Morente\, and Claude Delangle. His career has taken him across Spain\,\nPortugal\, Denmark\, Germany\, the United States\, and the United Arab Emirates\, and he has\nparticipated in more than 30 album recordings.\nHis distinctions include the 2015 Culture Award from the City Council of Torredelcampo\, Best\nAlbum at the 2016 Jaén Music Awards\, the 2018 Musical Excellence Award from Diario Jaén\, the\n2024 Jaén Public Employment Award\, and the 2026 Grajo Award for Modern Music and Other\nStyles. \n \n 
URL:https://thespaceforart.com/event/the-space-for-music-jose-bevia-trio-with-special-guest-sergio-albacete/
LOCATION:the Space\, 356 Bloomfield Ave\, Montclair\, New Jersey\, 07042
CATEGORIES:Music
